with q as (
  select $l23_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The chapter says mission architecture is where 'the rocket equation stops being physics and becomes design.' What is the single principle underlying all good architecture?", "options": ["Always use the largest rocket available", "Never carry mass through a velocity change it does not need to experience \u2014 arrange the journey so each parcel of mass sees only the \u0394v it must, and no more", "Always land the whole spacecraft to keep the design simple", "Minimize the number of astronauts to save mass"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Because the rocket equation is exponential, small differences in how a mission is structured produce enormous differences in launch mass \u2014 so architecture is where that leverage is tamed or unleashed. The taming principle, first met as staging, is to never carry mass through a \u0394v it doesn't need: drop empty tanks (staging), leave the return vehicle in orbit (lunar-orbit rendezvous), make propellant at the destination (ISRU). Each is the same idea applied to the whole shape of a journey, and it's why architecture must be chosen before any hardware \u2014 it sets the mass requirements for everything else."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why does lunar-orbit rendezvous (LOR) let an Apollo mission fit on a single Saturn V, when direct ascent needed the never-built Nova?", "options": ["LOR uses a more powerful engine", "LOR leaves the heavy return vehicle (return propellant + heat shield) in lunar orbit, so only a stripped-down ~15 t lander is landed and only its ~4.7 t ascent stage returns \u2014 removing the most expensive mass (landed-and-returned) from the descent and ascent, a saving that compounds all the way back to the pad", "LOR skips lunar orbit entirely and flies straight to the surface", "LOR carries no return propellant at all"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The heavy Command and Service Module \u2014 the crew's ride home \u2014 never needs