s": ["Descent is the biggest cost, and the Moon has no gravity", "Launch to low Earth orbit is more than half the whole budget ('LEO is halfway to anywhere'), and there is no line for Earth-return braking because the atmosphere donates that huge deceleration for free", "Every maneuver costs the same, so architecture doesn't matter", "The budget is dominated by trans-earth injection"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Launch alone (~9,400 of ~18,200 m/s) is more than the entire round trip to the surface and back combined \u2014 the tyranny of Earth's gravity well, and why once you're in orbit you've spent more than half your total \u0394v. And the budget is only ~18,200 m/s because Earth's atmosphere brakes the ~11 km/s return for free (converting it to heat against the shield); without that gift the budget would nearly double. The \u0394v budget is the master tool because every architectural trick \u2014 staging, LOR, aerobraking, ISRU \u2014 either removes a line or keeps expensive mass off one."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Rendezvous made LOR possible but risky, and its orbital mechanics are counterintuitive. To catch up with a target ahead of you in orbit, what must you do \u2014 and why did NASA fly Gemini before Apollo?", "options": ["Speed up (thrust toward the target); Gemini tested engines", "Slow down \u2014 thrusting forward raises your orbit, which by Kepler's laws makes you move slower and fall behind, so you must drop to a lower, faster orbit to catch up from below; Gemini existed largely to prove rendezvous and docking in Earth orbit before betting lives on it at the Moon", "Fire sideways to change planes; Gemini tested spacesuits", "Wait passively for the orbits to align; Gemini tested reentry"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "In orbit, speeding up raises you into a higher, slower orbit and you fall behind \u2014 so to catch a target ahead you slow down, drop lower and faster, close from below, then rise to match. This is genuinely counterintuitive, and because LOR's whole success hinged on the ascent stage finding and docking with the waiting Command Module (fail, and the crew is stranded with no rescue), NASA could not risk trying it first at the Moon. The Gemini program de-risked it in Earth orbit \u2014 first rendezvous Dec 1965, first docking March 1966. LOR traded that catastrophic-but-managed risk for the decisive mass savings that met the deadline."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "Explain how lunar-orbit rendezvous is 'the staging principle applied to the surface.' State the staging principle, identify what mass is 'dropped' (and to where) in LOR and why, and give one more example of the same principle from the course \u2014 including the Mars case.", "rubric": "THE STAGING PRINCIPLE (from Unit I / L3): drop mass the moment it is no longer needed \u2014 empty fuel tanks, spent engines \u2014 so you do not waste propellant accelerating that dead mass through the LATER velocity changes. Stated generally: never carry mass through a \u0394v it does not need to experience. LOR AS STAGING APPLIED TO THE SURFACE: the heavy Command and Service Module \u2014 carrying the return propellant, the Earth-entry heat shield, and the journey-home life support \u2014 is 'dropped' in the sense of being LEFT IN LUNAR ORBIT rather than landed, so it never experiences the lunar DESCENT (~2,000 m/s) and ASCENT (~1,900 m/s) velocity changes. Its return equipment is only needed for the trip home, not on the surface, so making it descend and re-ascend would be carrying mass through \u0394v it does not need \u2014 exactly what staging forbids. Only a stripped-down Lunar Module (no heat shield, no Earth-return propellant, minimal life support) descends and returns, and even it drops its descent stage on the Moon before ascent (no point lifting the landing legs back up). So LOR 'stages' the mission SPATIALLY: the part that doesn't need to make the trip down is separated and parked in orbit. WHY THIS SAVES SO MUCH: landed-and-returned mass is the most leveraged in the mission (it compounds through the rocket equation backward from the surface to the pad), so removing the ~30 t CSM from the descent/ascent saves far more than 30 t of launch mass \u2014 enough to fit one Saturn V instead of the never-built Nova. ANOTHER EXAMPLE / MARS: in-situ resource utilization on Mars is the same principle \u2014 rather than carry the return propellant all the way to Mars and land it (paying the full compounding penalty) just to launch it back, you MANUFACTURE it on the surface from the atmosphere's CO2 and the soil's water ice, so you never launch it from Earth, send it across space, or land it. (Also acceptable: dropping spent booster stages during launch; leaving the LM descent stage on the Moon; aerobraking, which lets the atmosphere 'pay' a braking \u0394v for free.) A strong answer names the unifying idea \u2014 never carry mass through a velocity change it does not need to experience \u2014 and shows it running from staging to LOR to Martian propellant."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "Apply the architecture logic forward to a crewed Mars mission. Explain why the LOR-style insight is even more compelling at Mars, how the atmosphere and in-situ resource utilization each reshape the budget, why pre-positioning helps, and how it all ties to the through-line of the unit.", "rubric": "WHY LOR-STYLE IS EVEN MORE COMPELLING AT MARS: Mars has a DEEPER gravity well than the Moon and the journey is vastly longer, so the compounding cost of landed-and-returned mass is even MORE severe \u2014 landing the whole return vehicle on Mars and launching it back would be even more ruinous than at the Moon. So leaving the heavy return vehicle in Mars orbit and landing only a minimal vehicle saves even more; the Houbolt insight (don't land what doesn't need to land) applies with a vengeance. THE ATMOSPHERE \u2014 FREE DECELERATION: Mars has a (thin) atmosphere, so an arriving spacecraft can shed velocity by DRAG \u2014 aerobraking / aerocapture \u2014 instead of burning propellant to slow down. This is a large 'free' \u0394v (the atmosphere does the braking, converting energy to heat), impossible at the airless Moon where all deceleration must be propulsive. (The same atmosphere that complicates landing, by heating the vehicle, also donates the drag.) ISRU \u2014 THE MARS EQUIVALENT OF LOR: the return propellant is among the most expensive mass to carry, because (by the compounding logic) it must be launched from Earth, sent to Mars, AND landed, just to be used to launch the crew back. In-situ resource utilization MANUFACTURES the return propellant (methane + oxygen) ON MARS from the atmosphere's carbon dioxide and the soil's water ice, so you never launch it from Earth, never send it across space, never land it \u2014 breaking the worst of the rocket-equation compounding, exactly as LOR avoided landing the heavy return vehicle. PRE-POSITIONING: because a Mars mission is so massive, send the heavy pieces \u2014 return vehicle, habitat, ISRU plant, supplies \u2014 AHEAD of the crew on slow, fuel-efficient, uncrewed flights, so the crew departs lighter with a fuelled ride home already waiting; this splits the mission across multiple launches and years, dramatically reducing PEAK mass. (Robert Zubrin's 'Mars Direct' leans hard on ISRU + pre-positioning; other schemes add Mars-orbit rendezvous and aerocapture.) THE THROUGH-LINE: never send a human where a machine would do unless what the human brings is worth the enormous price \u2014 and mission architecture is the art of paying that price CLEVERLY, arranging the journey so the most demanding cargo there is (the human payload, L19) is delivered and returned through as few expensive velocity changes as possible. A strong answer stresses that every serious Mars architecture is the same exercise Houbolt performed for the Moon: structure the journey so mass is carried through each \u0394v only when it must